
AMISOR borehole AM05 MICROCAT data for 2012/2013 season
-------------------------------------------------------

Data from the third download.

ascii format
------------
contained in column formatted files catxxxx_3.dat, where
xxxx = microcat serial number. The columns are:

1. decimal time - decimal days, where 0.0 = 0000 on January 1st 2001.
So, for example, midday on 15th January 2001 is decimal time 14.5
2. temperature (deg. C, T90)
3. conductivity (mS/cm)
4. pressure (dbar)
5. salinity (PSS78)
6. to 11. day, month, year, hour, minute, second.

* Note that all times are UTC.

* There is a line of data for each recording interval (i.e. no gaps),
and null data is represented by -999.0

* Clock errors were small (< 20 seconds), and no clock drift corrections have 
been made.

* Mean pressures over this latest download (including all pressure steps):

cat7327 =  580.2 dbar (i.e. point value immediately after deployment in December 2009)
cat7326 =  701.5 dbar
cat7322 =  838.9 dbar

* The pressure steps appear to be real.

* Original pressure data for microcat 7327 are bad. All conductivity and
salinity values are recalculated using a constant pressure value of 580.2 dbar
(the value immediately after deployment in December 2009).  Unexpected salinity/conductivity trends
are evident in the recalculated data, most likely related to real values measured by a frozen-in 
instrument. Note that the other 2 microcats show rapid pressure change events - with no
pressure data for cat7327, it is unknown if this instrument underwent the same changes. As a
result, there may be a further inaccuracy in the recalculated salinity data for cat7327
of up to 0.03 (PSS78) (corresponding to pressure out by 50 dbar).


